  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and created the rule, and both exist in Kibana. The rule is switched on, but I couldn't confirm that its 5xx filter matches any documents (details at the end).\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each record is one web request, with:\n- the response code (`response`, stored as text, not a number)\n- the size of the response (`bytes`)\n- the client's address (`clientip`)\n- the page requested (`url` / `request`), plus `extension` and `referer`\n- where the request came from and went to (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`)\n- the client's browser and operating system (`agent`, `machine.os`)\n- `host`, `tags`, `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-721616a7 Web logs overview\"** ([open](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-721616a7-web-logs-overview-790cf3fb812e)) has 7 panels:\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Share of each response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and bytes\n- Top 10 source countries\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-721616a7 5xx spike\"** (id `66480a57-ac4b-4639-814f-37b75c4d3973`):\n- **Type:** Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`\n- **What it counts:** requests whose response code starts with \"5\" (`prefix` on `response.keyword`)\n- **When it fires:** more than 10 matching requests within 5 minutes\n- **Schedule:** every minute, tagged `EFF-721616a7` and `web-logs`\n\nKibana creates rules switched off, so I turned this one on; otherwise it would never check. The rule has no notification action, so when it fires the alert shows in Kibana but nobody is notified.\n\n**Not checked:** I tried to count the 5xx documents to confirm the filter works, but this Kibana setup blocks that kind of direct query. If the response codes are stored as plain three-digit values like `503`, the filter is correct.",
